Satisfying Demographic Parity
The Monte-Carlo sampling-based algorithm proposed in the previ-
ous section, while being efficient, returns a fair 𝜀-net, which is larger
than standard 𝜀-nets by roughly a log(𝑘)factor. Besides, similar to
other randomized Monte-Carlo algorithms, it does not always find
a valid solution. In this section, we propose two fair discrepancy-
based algorithms to address these issues; we propose deterministic
methods that construct smaller fair 𝜀-nets. Such results come at
the cost of increased running time. While both discrepancy-based
algorithms return a fair 𝜀-net of (asymptotically) the same size, the
first one is more efficient when 𝑚is small and 𝑑is large, while the
second one is more efficient when 𝑑is small and 𝑚is large.
4.2.1 Discrepancy-based algorithm for fair 𝜀-net. We first describe
the notion of discrepancy to construct an 𝜀-net. After that, we adjust
this method to construct a fair𝜀-net. More details on the definitions
and the proofs relating to the standard 𝜀-net construction using
discrepancy can be found in [25, 44].
Before we start the description of our algorithm, we give some
useful definitions. Let (𝑋,R)be a range space with |𝑋|=𝑛and
|R|=𝑚. Define a coloring function 𝜅:𝑋→{− 1,+1}that as-
signs a color+1or−1to each point in 𝑋. The coloring 𝜅differs
from the demographic groups and should not be mistaken with
colors/groups 𝐶={𝑐1,𝑐2,···,𝑐𝑘}. The discrepancy of𝜅over a
range𝑅∈Ris defined as:|𝜅(𝑅)|=|︁|︁|︁∑︁
𝑝∈𝑅𝜅(𝑝)|︁|︁|︁. The discrepancy
of the function 𝜅is defined as: disc(𝜅)=max𝑅∈R|𝜅(𝑅)|. Namely,
it is the color difference in the most unbalanced range based on
coloring𝜅(·). The discrepancy of the range space (𝑋,R)is de-
fined as the best achievable discrepancy by a coloring function,
disc(𝑋)=min𝜅:𝑋→{− 1,+1}disc(𝜅).
Amatching Πof𝑋is a set of pairs{(𝑝𝑖,𝑝𝑗)|𝑝𝑖,𝑝𝑗∈𝑋}that
partitions the entire set 𝑋into𝑛/2pairs. A coloring 𝜅is said to be
compatible with a matching Π, if for any pair(𝑝𝑖,𝑝𝑗)∈Π, we have:
𝜅(𝑝𝑖)+𝜅(𝑝𝑗)=0.
The following procedure defines a major step in building 𝜀-nets
using this method:
Random Halving .LetΠbe an arbitrary matching on 𝑋. For each
pair(𝑝𝑖,𝑝𝑗)∈Π, randomly either color 𝑝𝑖as+1and𝑝𝑗as−1, or
the other way around, by tossing a fair coin. Then, without loss
of generality, only keep the points with color +1. Let𝑋(1)be the
result set with|𝑋(1)|=𝑛
