4.3 Satisfying Custom-Ratio Fairness
In this section, we discuss the second, more general, fairness defi-
nition where the user provides the ratios T={𝜏1,𝜏2,···,𝜏𝑘}and
requires the returned 𝜀-netSto satisfy|S𝑐𝑖|
|S|=𝜏𝑖for every𝑐𝑖∈𝐶.
Through the whole subsection, we assume that we have access
to the rangesR; in other words, the ranges are explicitly given to
us as input. This assumption is valid in most cases; for example,
the discrepancy approach to deterministically find an 𝜀-net is based
on this assumption [ 25,44]. Assuming that we have access to the
rangesRand|R|=𝑚, finding an 𝜀-net of the range space (𝑋,R)
can be reduced to the hitting set problem. In other words, based on
the Definition 1,Nis an𝜀-net of(𝑋,R), if and only if it hits all the
ranges inR𝜀={𝑅|𝑅∈R,|𝑅|
|𝑋|≥𝜀}.Given the range space (𝑋,R), one can first filter out the light
ranges and keep only the heavy ranges, akaR𝜀. Then, compute a
hitting set for the new range space (𝑋,R𝜀). Any algorithm solving
the hitting set problem would result in solving the 𝜀-net problem.
𝜀-netreduction−→
overR𝜀Hitting Set
In the fairness context, any fair hitting set given the constraints
Tis also a fair 𝜀-net. Solving the Fair Hitting Set problem is dis-
cussed in [ 31] in the general setting. They provide a randomized
𝑂(log𝑛)-approximation algorithm for the fair set cover that runs
in𝑂(poly(𝑛,𝑚))time. The approximation factor holds in expec-
tation. The algorithm in [ 31] is a greedy algorithm that picks the
most promising points at each step. As a result, by applying the
same algorithm, we can compute a fair𝜀-net for the range space
(𝑋,R)with expected size 𝑂(OPT·log𝑛), where OPT is the fair
𝜀-net with the minimum size. We note that this algorithm does not
find a fair𝜀-net with an absolute size as we had in Theorems 6, 13,
and 17. Instead, our algorithm for custom-ratios returns a fair 𝜀-net
with size larger by an (expected) 𝑂(log𝑛)factor from the smallest
possible fair 𝜀-net with respect to the CR constraints T.
Theorem 18. Given a range space (𝑋,R), with|𝑋|=𝑛,|R|=𝑚,
CR constraintsT, and a parameter 𝜀∈ (0,1), there exists a ran-
domized algorithm that constructs a fair 𝜀-net with respect to T, of
expected size 𝑂(OPT·log(𝑛))in𝑂(poly(𝑛,𝑚))time, where OPT is
the size of the smallest fair 𝜀-net with respect to T.
The algorithm from Theorem 18 works in general range spaces;
however, assuming a range space (𝑋,R)with a bounded VC-dim
would result in better approximation algorithms [ 22,27,40,56]. The
